      "snippet": {
        "text": "Fourth, and related, it is well-known that parents from disadvantaged backgrounds are less likely to help in their child's education (see Mayer et al. 2019 on parent-child reading). The problem can be particularly acute where parents migrate for work, and this disadvantage is well recognized by the Shaoyang education authorities who provided us with their database of left-behind children. 3 Clearly it is challenging for government and schools to try to engage absent parents using conventional methods, such as teacher-parents committees or meetings, but frequent texting of feedback results is possible, and our study permits assessment of whether this is effective. \n\nOur experimental results indicate that frequent scorecard-style informational feedback produces positive and persistent cognitive effects for younger Grade 3 students (average age 9) but not for older Grade 5 students (average age 11). For the younger students, the first-stage feedback discussions with the students alone raise math and language scores by around 0.15σ. The second stage, the family bridge, produces a further improvement especially for math ranging from 0.17σ for non-leftbehind students to 0.26σ for the left-behind. Interestingly, we also find the math improvements persist well especially for the not left-behind pupils, when they are re-tested around 30 months after the intervention, as they are passing out of Grade 6. \n\nAt the same time, we find that starting the feedback process 2 years later, in Grade 5, has zero effect, whether for student-only feedback or at the further stage to parents -whether pupils are left-behind or not. This negative result fits with the Heckman and Kautz (2013, 58) view that 'prevention is more effective than remediation'. The implication is that 11-year-olds in Grade 5 -almost adolescent -need much more intensive tutoring than 9 year olds.",
