      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) literacy is a primary objective in the field of education globally (Stolpe & Hallström, 2024;Yim & Su, 2024). In accordance with Naqvi (2020), AI is briefly defined as the sub-division of computer science that covers the imitation of intelligent behaviour in computers and their capacity to mimic, and ideally enhance human behaviour. However, the definition of AI is highly contested, and little consensus has been drawn across the range of fields in which the terminology is used (Kelly et al., 2023). As revealed by Jatileni et al. (2023), the use of AI has transformed the educational landscape. AI possesses a wide range of educational applications, such as tailored learning platforms aimed at facilitating learners' educational advancement (Chan, 2023;Sanusi, Ayanwale, & Chiu, 2024), automated assessment systems designed to assist educators (Naqvi, 2020), and facial recognition systems employed to gain insights into learners' behaviours (Dai et al., 2024). According to researchers, formal education from K-12 should prioritize teaching children how to engage with AI and focus on AI literacy (Ayanwale et al., 2022;Chiu, 2024). As a result, AI has lately been included into K-12 curricula, putting additional pressure on key players, particularly educators who direct the process of teaching and learning. Researchers do, however, affirm that it is difficult for educators and educational institutions to incorporate classical AI and computational thinking into the K-12 curriculum. This is particularly true in South African schools. The term \"K-12\", according to Ayanwale et al. (2022), refers to a broad range of students and distinct educational goals for each age group. Kindergarteners, for instance, concentrate on mastering fundamental math and reading abilities. Seniors in high school frequently focus on mathematical modelling and employ sophisticated problem-solving strategies to get ready for college (Ayanwale et al., 2022;Li & Gu, 2023). There are significant differences in the materials and teaching strategies that are most effective for different age groups, categories, learning environments, and expected learning objectives.",
