        "text": "In this paper, we report descriptive findings from a large-scale observational study of the classroom experiences of individual students using an observational tool, Optimizing Learning Opportunities for Students (OLOS), derived from the Individualizing Student Instruction (ISI) observational measure (Connor, Morrison, et al., 2009). This study adds to the literature in terms of its scale (1,404 children observed for multiple hours on 3 days across the school years), geographic diversity (six US states), and detailed descriptions of the content, context, and management of literacy activities in preschool through third-grade classrooms. \n\nThe following research questions are addressed: \n\n( ) and community-based preschools (n = 43). Typically, preschool classrooms had a lead teacher and at least one instructional aide. The kindergarten through third-grade classrooms were general education classrooms located at public elementary schools (n = 60) and private elementary schools (n = 9). In addition, seven private kindergarten classrooms were located at community-based child care centers. The school-level percentage of elementary students qualifying for free or reduced lunch averaged 68%. \n\nStudent demographics can be found in Table 1. As shown in the table, the number of students recruited was not uniform across grade levels. This variability reflects an intentional over-recruitment of preschool and kindergarten classrooms because of the focus of the Early Learning Network on studying learning opportunities in these classrooms. Students with an IEP were not explicitly excluded from the study as long as they were in the classroom during the observation. This study was approved by the University of California, Irvine Institutional Review Board and the study conforms to the U.S. Federal Policy for the Protection of Human Subjects. Parental informed consent was obtained for observed students in each class and each student gave their assent prior to the