      "snippet": {
        "text": "Automated writing evaluation. PaperRater (2020), an online automated writing evaluation website, was used to generate the AWE feedback on participants' writing. After agreeing to terms of service, the free standard version of PaperRater allows analysis of writing after specifying the writer's education level, type of paper, and whether plagiarism detection is needed. A user only needs to paste in a text and then click \"get report\" to receive immediate feedback (see Fig. 1). After processing the text, PaperRater provides feedback on grammar and spelling (see Fig. 2 in system feedback and Fig. 3 for generated report feedback), vocabulary   usage, sentence length and variability, phrases to avoid, passive voice detection, readability statistics, and analysis of sentence beginnings. PaperRater uses not only words but also bar graphs to explain and provide this feedback. In addition, \"a score that reflects…what…[a writer] may receive from [a]…teacher\" is also given (PaperRater, 2020). In what follows, detailed information regarding each type of automated feedback is elaborated. For plagiarism detection, PaperRater scans quotations to determine the level of originality of a text, which is presented as a percentage (see Fig. 4). Additionally, this function highlights and comments on all unoriginal materials and provides links to files that contain content like that in the text. The grammar and spell check function involves marking spelling and grammar errors identified in a document in bold, providing correction suggestions for these errors, and elaborating on the nature of these errors. Writing feedback and instructions are provided with respect to word choice, vocabulary, and style (i.e., use of transitional words, information on sentence length, readability indices, sentence beginnings, and passive voice). \n\nFor word choice, PaperRater identifies outdated, inappropriate, or clichéd words and phrases; a score for inappropriate phrasing is calculated and displayed next to a numerical figure for comparison of the vocabulary in texts by authors of the same education level. A list of inappropriate words arranged in order from most to least inappropriate is provided, allowing the author to reconsider the word choice and replace words with alternatives from the built-in thesaurus.",
