      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) has quickly emerged as a disruptive force in many sectors of the economy, including education. The capacity of generative AI (GAI) tools such as ChatGPT and Bard to produce language that resembles that of a human has made them popular in recent years. This means that there is a great deal of promise for improving learning experiences and making it easier to acquire complex knowledge (Dwivedi et al., 2023). These advanced language model-powered tools are increasingly being incorporated into curricula, enabling automated assessment, individualized learning, and content production help. Although artificial intelligence (AI) has many advantages for education, it is crucial to identify and resolve any potential biases and limits in these technologies. The fact that GAI techniques rely on large datasets with potential biases that could unintentionally be reinforced in the created content is one of their main limitations. This may result in the spread of false information or cultural insensitivity, which is especially problematic in educational contexts where inclusivity and truth are crucial. Teachers may find it challenging to fully accept or comprehend AI-generated outputs because of the opaque nature of AI decision-making processes, sometimes known as the 'black box' problem. This makes it difficult to grasp how these tools arrive at their findings. \n\nGenerative artificial intelligence (GAI) tools such as ChatGPT and Bard, which are based on large language models (LLMs), use deep learning to process and comprehend human language (Shen et al., 2023). These models can understand syntax and context, and they can even produce text responses resembling those of humans because they are trained on enormous volumes of textual data. They are now more crucial than ever for various NLP activities, including chatbots, sentiment analysis, text summarization, and language translation. GAI tools' ability to produce coherent and contextually appropriate text makes them essential for automating language-based tasks and improving human-computer interactions (Abd-Alrazaq et al., 2023). They can continuously advance and change to meet new problems because of their capacity to learn from data. LLMs use their text-generating capabilities to enable generative AI systems (Feuerriegel et al., 2023).",
