        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "In recent years, there has been an increase in the adoption of artificial intelligence (AI), including generative AI, in everyday life and industry. Generative AI is defined as 'computational techniques that are capable of generating seemingly new, meaningful content such as text, images, or audio from training data' (Feuerriegel et al. 2024, p. 111). Generative AI can rapidly generate coherent and contextually relevant materials, based on a given prompt for almost any topic and in a range of writing styles. A wide range of generative AI technologies is now available, with platforms such as ChatGPT, Copilot, DeepSeek, Gemini, DALL-E, and Midjourney being just some examples of the tools that have already changed (and continue to change) different domains of life, work, and education. \n\nGenerative AI is poised to significantly enhance both language learning and teaching practices (Agustini 2023;Ali et al. 2023;Creely 2024;Mohamed 2024;Creely et al. 2025). \n\nWhile generative AI has the potential to positively impact language learning and optimise teaching effectiveness, its potential is realised in and through quality pedagogical decision-making (Jeon and Lee 2023). Furthermore, teachers' pedagogical decision-making, particularly when utilising technology, is heavily influenced by relational and contextual factors (Fawns 2022). As a result, there are concerns that the integration of generative AI 'into the pedagogy of schools degrades the relationship between teachers and students' and could enforce a formulaic model of instruction, distort curriculum and invite bias into decisions regarding student achievement (Williamson et al. 2024). With public concerns about the adoption and use of generative AI in education (Williamson et al. 2024), teachers must decide how they will navigate relational (e.g. teacher-student relationships and collegial relationships) and contextual (e.g. instructional models and policies) factors in their pedagogical decision-making. \n\nThis article, therefore, examines the perspectives and experiences of ten English as an Additional Language (EAL) teachers in Australia as they consider if and/or how to use generative AI in their adult teaching contexts.",
