      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) has seen an accelerated integration into higher education, revolutionizing teaching, learning, and administrative processes. AI-based tools such as intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) and AI-driven chatbots provide students with real-time, personalized feedback and assistance [ 1 , 2 ]. These technologies adapt to students' learning styles, allowing for customized educational experiences that were previously difficult to achieve in traditional settings. AI can also enhance language learning, as seen in its applications for writing assistance, reading comprehension, and vocabulary acquisition [ 3 , 4 ]. AI-powered grading systems can evaluate large volumes of assignments and exams, reducing the workload on educators while ensuring consistency in grading. Automated essay scoring systems have been implemented to assess students' writing, providing instant feedback to improve their academic performance [ 5 ]. AI has also streamlined administrative processes in higher education institutions. Universities utilize AI-driven virtual assistants to handle student inquiries, assist with course registration, and provide academic counseling. For example, AI-powered chatbots, such as IBM's Watson at Deakin University, offer round-the-clock assistance to students, reducing administrative burdens on human staff [ 4 ]. However, this integration is not without significant challenges and ethical concerns. Issues such as data privacy [ 6 ], algorithmic bias [ 7 ], academic integrity [ 8 ], overdependency [ 9 ], and transparency in AI-driven decision-making [ 10 ] raise critical questions that must be addressed to ensure responsible and equitable AI use in higher education settings. \n\nDespite some issues in using AI, it has provided students with new opportunities for academic assistance and skill development. A study by Chan and Hu [ 11 ] found that most university students viewed generative AI as a valuable tool for learning, particularly for tasks such as writing assistance, brainstorming, and research analysis. Similarly, Sami et al. [ 12 ] reported that medical students found AI tools to be effective and credible learning aids, helping them grasp complex medical concepts more efficiently than traditional study methods. Additionally, AI's role in enhancing creativity, productivity, and research capabilities has been well-documented, with students reporting increased efficiency in their academic tasks due to AI-generated insights and suggestions [ 11 ].",
