      "snippet": {
        "text": "Prompt engineering [Bach et al. 2022;Dang et al. 2022;Deckers et al. 2023;Hou et al. 2022;Jiang et al. 2022b;Liu and Chilton 2022], coined by Gwern Branwen in 20203 , describes the incremental and iterative process of crafting inputs (\"prompts\") for LLMs. LLMs commonly support conversational interaction: we give them a prompt, and they reply with a related text or image, plausibly derived from the prompt. To apply LLMs to specific technical tasks, however, an informal, simplistic approach to prompting usually leads to disappointing results. Effective prompts need to be structured to align with both the targeted task, and the LLM that will receive the prompt. \n\nPrompt engineering takes a systematic approach to creating prompts to convey tasks to pretrained LLMs [Beurer-Kellner et al. 2023;Reynolds and McDonell 2021]. To synthesize formally verified programs, prompts need to be engineered so that task descriptions are as unambiguous as possible [Bach et al. 2022;Dang et al. 2022], and solution examples must suit the target programming language (in our case Dafny). Prompt engineering techniques can also train LLMs explicitly, e.g. through few-shot learning with a limited number of example problems and solutions: in our domain, textual problem description, method signature, and test cases.",
