        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Since the advent of GPT-3.5 in 2022, AI has garnered huge traction among students, teachers, administrators, and researchers alike. This rise stems from the ease of generating diverse and sophisticated multimedia content using simple, natural language instructions, referred to as 'prompts'. The specificity and effectiveness of prompts strongly determine the quality of AI-generated output. Prompt engineering thus has caught huge traction, especially surrounding the use of AI in education settings. Prompt engineering is an empirical and emerging domain which deals with the ways of systematically structuring clear, concise, and effective prompts that enhance the quality of AI-generated content. Specifically, prompt engineering refers to the \"systematic design and optimization of input prompts to guide the responses of large language models, ensuring high levels of accuracy, relevance, coherence, and usability in the generated output\" [12]. With the growing capability and accessibility of emerging AI models, prompt engineering has been increasingly recognized as an essential competency for the future workforce, particularly in STEM. \n\nBroadly, prompt engineering techniques can be categorized into two types: foundational and advanced. Foundational techniques emphasize clarity, structure, and organization of prompts to optimize outputs. Advanced techniques, on the other hand, involve more sophisticated and dynamic interactions with the responses (such as directing the model 'to think step by step' or pretraining it with disciplinary information) to yield precise, accurate, and high-quality outputs. \n\nIn this study, we primarily focus on the foundational techniques and suggest readers [12] for the details on advanced techniques. Below, we briefly outline the foundational techniques to better contextualize discussions in the later sections of this manuscript. In contrast, general instructions often lead to overly broad or ambiguous responses given the model's vast training dataset. For example, if the objective is to gain a basic understanding of electrical resistance, the prompt \"Explain the meaning of R in science\" may yield broad or contextually irrelevant results.",
