          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19422, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study investigated the extent to which the GenAI assistant tool, CyberScholar, can support K-12 students' writing in diverse subject areas. The findings indicate that participating students perceived the tool as a valuable help in their writing development, especially in receiving detailed and specific feedback connected to their teachers' rubric, engaging in interactive clarification, and developing their writing quality. \n\nIn correlation with recent studies on the practical implementation of GenAI Tools in writing (Godwin-Jones, 2022;Ghufron, 2019;Dembsey, 2017;Saeed, 2024), participants in this study also observed grammar improvements and corrections in their texts. However, unlike other tools being studied, CyberScholar incorporated teaching resources, such as books used by the teacher and PowerPoint slides, to supplement the AI feedback provided to students. This made the feedback more closely aligned with educational standards and teacher expectations across different subject areas. \n\nAlso, assessment criteria were more transparent and familiar to students. \n\nThe provision of detailed, specific, and timely feedback in the students' texts, reflects the key aspects of formative assessment discussed by Cope & Kalantzis (2015), Chan & Hu (2023), and, Lee & Moore (2024). Similar to findings by Wan and Chen (2023) and Steiss et al. (2023) the participants also evaluated the quality of the AI feedback, where they point out as actionable, especially when feedback pinpointed specific areas for revision and offered examples of improved phrasing. Some students re-submitted revised drafts to observe improvements in their rubric scores, tracked their progress demonstrating iterative engagement that aligns with the idea of the AI and human feedback learning model by Cope & Kalantzis (2019, 2023). This model highlights the combination of formative assessment, cycles of cognition, and metacognition (reflecting on the writing process). \n\nStudents also appreciated the possibility to ask follow-up questions in CyberScholar, a feature that distinguished the tool from other static feedback tools. \n\nThis functionally potentially increases student agency and creativity during the revision process.",
