      "snippet": {
        "text": "In the field of education, assessing students' activities of various types is an integral part of teachers' workload. However, traditional methods of scoring assignments or exams and providing constructive feedback have often been described as labor-intensive, time-consuming, and subject to various biases (Warschauer & Ware, 2006). The situation is particularly aggravated in writing classes where teachers constantly worry about scoring fairly and objectively and providing effective remarks (Fathman & Walley, 1990). In support of this activity, the use of computer-assisted assessment systems, particularly automated writing evaluation (AWE) software, has increased exponentially (Wilson & Czik, 2016). In this paper I construe writing assessment through the lens of Activity Theory (Engestr€ om, 1999), which defines any such scenario as goal-oriented and tool-mediated: its core is a subject (here teacher), an object (here student writing assessment), and a tool (here teacher knowledge, now assisted or even replaced by AWE). \n\nUp until 2022, the capability of computers to realistically assist teachers in the full task of providing grading and feedback on writing was seriously limited. However, with the publication in November 2022 of ChatGPT, evidencing a quantum leap in the capability of artificial intelligence (AI) to perform such tasks, the integration of AI-powered assessment software into educational contexts has begun to revolutionize the assessment process, particularly for writing instructors. It promises them solutions for providing immediate and higher-level feedback on language and content in addition to automated scoring, thus alleviating teachers' workload by minimizing the time spent by teachers on assessing and enabling them to focus more on teaching (Balla, 2023). \n\nAlthough general AI resources such as ChatGPT can themselves be directly used to grade, and provide other feedback on, student writing, a number of AI-powered writing assessment packages are now available which provide a more friendly dedicated interface for the teacher. They include the latest versions of Grammarly, Turnitin, ProWritingAid, Quillbot, and CoGrader (S€ uveges, 2024).",
