      "snippet": {
        "text": "A brief review of previous review studies on language learning with AI chatbots is provided in this section, along with the associated limitations. Many review studies aim to summarize past work and guide future trends, focusing on assessing the effectiveness of using chatbots in language education (Huang et al., 2022;Klimova & Seraj, 2023;Lo et al., 2024;Zhang et al., 2023aZhang et al., , 2023b)). For example, Huang and colleagues (2022) Fig. 1 AT framework adapted from Engeström (1987) conducted a systematic analysis of 25 empirical studies to analyze the benefits and limitations of using chatbots in L1, L2, and EFL learning. They recognized the technological and pedagogical aspects of chatbots and how students use them in their learning process. Lo and colleagues (2024) conducted a systematic review on the application of Chat-GPT in ESL/EFL context, emphasizing the affordances of ChatGPT, such as increasing learning opportunities and personalized learning, in different domains (e.g. writing, reading, etc.) of ESL/EFL education. However, these review papers failed to include the unique student learning outcomes, which weakened the assessment of the chatbots' usefulness in language instruction. \n\nMeanwhile, it has been recognized that the effectiveness of student learning with AI chatbots could be affected by many factors (e.g., teachers, instructional design, etc.) (Chiu et al., 2023;Chiu, 2024Chiu, , 2025;;Heung & Chiu, 2025;Yang & Kyun, 2022). Several review studies have attempted to offer insights on how to establish an optimal AI language learning environment to enhance student learning. For example, Chiu (2023Chiu ( , 2024Chiu ( , 2025) ) used the First Principles of Instruction to examine the instructional designs in 83 selected articles. They demonstrated beneficial impact on language learning and shed light on teachers' roles in enhancing student learning outcomes. Yang and Kyun (2022) specified learning outcomes and tried to guide the future direction of AI technology in language education from the AT perspective by conducting a systematic review of 25 articles published from January 2007 to February 2021.",
