      "snippet": {
        "text": "Despite the growing recognition of digital well-being as a critical educational outcome, a significant gap remains in understanding how different types of literacy contribute to students' digital well-being across various age groups and socioeconomic contexts. International educational policies increasingly emphasize digital competence; however, implementation varies widely across different educational systems [18]. School-age students, as a group that extensively uses digital technologies, need to take advantage of their opportunities and be protected from potential risks. As media and technology do not go away, the need to develop children's social and emotional learning skills at an early age will continue [19]. Digital technologies are becoming increasingly important in the daily lives of children and youth as part of meaningful leisure activities such as communication, socialization, play, and learning [20]. However, overuse or addiction to digital technologies by children and adolescents (0-18 years) has become a global concern [21]. Therefore, it is important to develop school-age students' literacy skills and support their digital well-being to enable them to become critical and active users of technology.",
