        "text": "To summarize, the results suggest that, a differentiated literacy instruction [1; 13; 19; 22], an explicit writing and vocabulary instruction [9; 17; 23], enrichment [18], and reading ability grouping in elementary and middle school [e.g., 16] are promising and could be beneficial to students with advanced literacy skills, but more research is needed. \n\nIncluded studies have an emphasis on the earlier school years, which call for more research to be conducted at middle school level. The reason for the heightened attention to reading and to the early school years, may be related to students' varying reading abilities seem to be more apparent during the early years than later on. \n\nTeachers are generally facing challenges in terms of knowledge, time, and resources. Efficient teacher training that addresses the needs of SALS seems to be crucial, and the issue of resources cannot be neglected. The following implications for future research have been identified during the present review: \n\n(1) the need for further research at the middle school level, and also at both school levels, in a larger variety of educational contexts, (2) findings call for increased scientific knowledge in the field of writing regarding as well characteristics and development, as instruction, (3) to examine if and how digitalization of literacy activities may change and/or complement previous knowledge of SALS and literacy instruction related to these students´ needs, ( 4) the results imply a potential research gap regarding the general academic achievement of SALS, and also on their attitudes towards learning and teaching, (5) the results signal that there is limited research which is based on a student perspective, and ( 6) identified models and methods could be of interest also in other countries, why it is motivated to perform research on these in a wider range of educational contexts.",
