      "snippet": {
        "text": "More studies have been done on e-rater such as the one made by Attali and Burstein [4] who investigate the application of the e-rater V.2 system for automated essay scoring, that was employed by the Educational Testing Service for automated essay scoring since 1999. According to their study, E-rater V.2 distinguishes itself from other scoring systems with its innovative features, including a concise and meaningful set of scoring criteria applicable to all prompts, a unified scoring model and standards, and transparent and flexible modeling procedures grounded in expert judgment. Their paper outlines the characteristics of this new system and provides evidence supporting the validity and reliability of its scoring outcomes. \n\nE-rater version 2.0 has been again investigated along with Criterion in a study by Burstein and Chodorow [10] in which they introduce the Criterion online writing service, showcasing its application of artificial intelligence in written content assessment. The authors highlight two main components: Critique Writing Analysis Tools, adept at detecting errors and identifying elements in essays, and e-rater version 2.0, an automated essay scoring system. Both provide personalized feedback to enhance student writing skills under the guidance of a classroom teacher. These tools, employing natural language processing and machine learning, surpass basic algorithms, occasionally aligning with human judges' evaluations as consistently as two human judges agree with each other. \n\nIn a related study, Ramineni et al. [11] employed the e-rater to analyze 4000 Test of English as a Foreign Language (TOEFL) essays, demonstrating a strong positive correlation and substantial agreement between automated and human scorings for both independent and integrated tasks. Similarly, Evanini et al. (2015) investigated 3385 secondary school students' TOEFL Junior Test responses, revealing a strong positive correlation and substantial agreement across all test items between automated and human scoring. \n\nExploring further tools in automated essay scoring (AES), Dikli [12] provides methods to enhance the reliability of AES systems, emphasizing the calibration with diverse essay samples. Dikli discusses the emergence of student-based tools like MY Access!® and CriterionSM, originally designed for large-scale essay scoring but currently utilized as writing tools in educational settings. The article anticipates the potential transformation of AES systems into a public utility for broader educational access.",
