      "snippet": {
        "text": "The survey showed that 60% of educators have incorporated artificial intelligence into their classrooms to strengthen and simplify their daily teaching duties [1]. \n\nStrong AI, powered by deep learning technologies, possesses robust capabilities in natural language understanding and generation, enabling it to rapidly create high-quality texts. By learning from vast datasets, it grasps the intricacies of language patterns and features, providing educators with precise and comprehensive linguistic knowledge and resources. These contributions are invaluable for refining curriculum development, devising instructional content, personalizing the approach to teaching, and accurately assessing students' learning processes and outcomes. The Quality Assurance Agency for Higher Education (QAA) in the UK suggests that AI can enhance educational productivity; its ability to create various formats of educational resources makes learning more accessible and adaptable [2]. \n\nRegarding EFL instruction, the traditional approach to vocabulary acquisition often involves learners setting specific vocabulary learning goals, such as aiming to remember a certain number of new words each day, and creating a detailed study plan, covering daily study time and specific content, with tools like flashcards to facilitate learning. Under the guidance of teachers, learners complete set learning tasks, solidifying new vocabulary through exercises and verbal expression. After some time, regular testing and evaluation help learners to receive timely feedback on their learning outcomes, allowing them to adjust their study plans and strategies to ensure their learning objectives are met. However, this process of intentionally memorizing a large number of new words might lead to an overwhelming memory burden which can cause stress for learners, making it difficult to maintain long-term memory and eventually leading to study fatigue. The dependence on rote learning over an extended period can also result in learning fatigue, impacting learners' enduring motivation, and potentially causing them to find the process tedious, lose interest, and consequently, affect their learning effectiveness. Moreover, a lack of effective self-assessment methods may make it difficult for learners to accurately gauge their progress in memorizing and applying new vocabulary. \n\nAI-powered gamified learning models align with the learning tendencies and habits of digital-age learners, greatly stimulating their interest and intrinsic motivation to learn. The integration of AI technology in EFL instruction allows students to extend their vocabulary learning beyond the classroom, using AI-powered language learning platforms and mobile language learning apps.",
