      "snippet": {
        "text": "Literature has extensively studied the association between reading motivations and reading performance, suggesting that reading motivations have a beneficial effect on reading comprehension. For example, Orellana et al. (2020) examined 1070 Chilean third, fourth, and fifth-graders enrolled in public and private schools and found that students' motivations to read at the beginning of the academic year was significantly associated with advances in reading comprehension. The results of a mixed-methods study conducted by Louick et al. (2016) on 112 struggling middle school readers in semi-urban and urban settings indicate that students with more motivation are plausible to perform better in reading comprehension than the rest. Identical results are also found in investigations (e.g., Wang et al., 2020). However, in the research by Wang et al. (2020), only intrinsic motivations were positively connected with reading comprehension. \n\nWhile its association between reading motivations and reading comprehension has been largely investigated, little is known pertaining to the impact of online reading motivations on reading comprehension. Forzani et al. (2020) examined the relationship between the Online Research and Comprehension Assessment (ORCA) and the Motivations for Online Reading Questionnaire (MORQ), disclosing that each of the MORQ elements, namely curiosity/value, self-efficacy, and self-improvement beliefs, were statistically associated with ORCA. In addition, this study suggests that future research should include a broader scope of online reading (Forzani et al., 2020).",
