        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a transformative force in society, impacting diverse domains of education, especially writing. Often considered an essential skill for college and employment readiness (National Commission on Writing, 2004), writing is crucial for engagement across disciplines, audiences, and is vital for participation in implementation has been inconsistent (Gurajala, 2020;Mahanan et al., 2021;Shepard, 2010). Recent studies point out that feedback generated by AI can help address this challenge by offering timely and personalized feedback to learners (Chan & Hu, 2023;Lee & Moore, 2024;Shi & Aryadoust, 2024). In this sense, Wan and Chen (2023) evaluated feedback provided by GPT-3.5 on middle school science writing. The study showed that students tended to rate the feedback by humans and GPT equally on correctness, but they all rated the feedback by GPT as more useful. These findings show the feasibility of using GenAI as an assistant to generate feedback for students. \n\nA study by Steiss et al. (2023) investigated the use of GenAI for feedback in the K-12 setting. The study examined the use of ChatGPT for offering formative feedback on students' drafts. 200 students from grades 6-12 in a school in the USA participated in this research. The study involved a comparison of the feedback quality provided by both humans and AI, assessing them based on five measures: 1. adherence to criteria, 2. clarity in directions for improvement, 3. accuracy, 4. prioritization of essential features, and 5. employing a supportive tone. The results showed that differences between ChatGPT and humans were modest when considering the overall quality of feedback and timesavings, but human feedback was better than ChatGPT feedback for ⅘ elements of formative feedback. \n\nAnother recently published case study by Zheldibayeva et al. (2025) examined the impact of GenAI in writing assignments and the provision of formative feedback in K-12 context. T