      "snippet": {
        "text": "The survey used in the present study was from Cheng and Zhang (2024). We adapted the survey to align with the context of using ChatGPT for writing engagement. This version, which incorporated slight modifications from Huang and Teng (2025), consisted of 17 carefully designed items (see appendix). The instrument was developed to provide a comprehensive understanding of how students engage with ChatGPT feedback across affective, behavioral, and cognitive dimensions during the writing process. These items were specifically tailored to measure engagement after receiving feedback from GenAI in the treatment group. \n\nThe survey was divided into three categories: Affective Engagement (5 items), Behavioral Engagement (6 items), and Cognitive Engagement (6 items). Affective engagement assessed students' emotional responses to ChatGPT feedback. Items included questions such as: \"I find ChatGPT feedback helpful and important for writing.\" \"I enjoy using ChatGPT feedback for writing.\" These questions aimed to capture students' feelings of enjoyment, value, and overall emotional connection to the feedback provided by ChatGPT. Behavioral engagement evaluated students' actions and behaviors in response to the feedback received. Questions included: \"I make revisions for my writing based on ChatGPT feedback.\" \"I take notes of ChatGPT feedback carefully for writing.\" These items aimed to measure how actively students incorporated ChatGPT feedback into their writing and whether they demonstrated deliberate effort in engaging with the feedback. \n\nCognitive engagement focused on students' mental processes while interacting with ChatGPT feedback. Questions included: \"I can notice ChatGPT feedback for writing.\" \n\n\"I can understand ChatGPT feedback for writing.\" These items assessed students' ability to comprehend, analyze, and apply the feedback effectively in their writing tasks. The survey was administered in Chinese to ensure clarity and accessibility for all participants. Responses were collected using a 6-point Likert scale, where 1 represented \"strongly disagree\" and 6 represented \"strongly agree.\" For the control group, a parallel version of the survey was used. In this version, references to ChatGPT feedback were replaced with peer feedback to evaluate engagement in a traditional feedback context. This approach ensured that both groups' engagement levels could be compared effectively while maintaining consistency in survey structure and design.",
