      "snippet": {
        "text": "This class-based writing project by a group of student junior colleagues follows the methodology and philosophy of collective writing that is now well established in the literature. The collective led by editors Xiyuan Zhang and Dong Li provide a useful framework within which to review latest developments of ai China and specifically chatbots, with a focus on ChatGPT-4. The essay developed within a course on knowledge economy that examines the form of knowledge socialism and both the contribution and the problems of OpenAI's ChatGPT-4. The essay was organized and developed without my direct involvement: one of the critical factors in collective writing is architecture or structure and the process of self-organization among peers. The essay is a valuable contribution to discussions of ai and gpt-4 because it represents a Chinese perspective. It could be argued that these 'digital natives' are more up to date in using the latest technology and therefore also provide a users' view. It is important that the journal bire provide its western readers with what is happening in China. \n\nThe piece begins with an introduction by Dong Li who provides appropriate background and sets out the structure of the essay. Xiyuan Zhang examines the relation of gpt-4 to knowledge socialism as a form of knowledge economy focusing on its public goods characteristics. Zhujun Jiang looks to the impacts in higher education against the general background of ai and Ayubu Ismail Ngao more broadly examines both the problems and prospects of ChatGPT. Dong Li and Cunchi Wang with a glance at the history of technology development in China examines the pathway China must take if it is to remain competitive, and Dan Liu discusses the possibility of a Chinese ChatGPT. The concluding remarks are made by Xiyuan Zhang. The result is impressive and useful especially for our western readers and the contributors raise important questions about the latest generative ai technology that already has had a huge impact on text-based contributions together with other 'plug-ins' will reconfigure aspects of higher education's contribution to the knowledge economy.",
