      "snippet": {
        "text": "Past studies demonstrate the potential and the limitations of AI for primary school learners to write. In a recent systematic review of the empirical research on K12 AI education, only 25 past studies published between 2018 and 2023 were identified. Only four focused on writing development 21 . Such scarcity prompts concern about ecological validity. Theoretical statements on AI writing are often ahead of classroom practice. There is little indication as to when such practice might be age-appropriate. To the extent that writing is reviewed, it is directed more to the sentence level than to organising discourse and that focus might themselves instead lead to more formulaic production. This is also consistent with the results of the meta-analysis on automated writing evaluation systems. Huang et al. 22 pooled 42 experimental contrasts and obtained an average medium effect (d = 0.67) for overall L2 writing quality. They indicated that the smallest advances were observed for primary school learners due to algorithmic mismatch with adolescent calibrated algorithms and with prefabricated linguistic repertoires of younger learners. A meta-analysis of treatments with ChatGPT also found a large overall effect size in favor of \"learning performance\" (g = 0.87) 23 . However, the researchers did not test the treatment by primary school learner moderator for lack of a large sample to reliably produce an effect estimate. It is a failure that contributes to the lack of research in the developmental period. \n\nSimilarly, qualitative assessments reflect these quantitative worries. Steiss et al. 24 analysed 200 similar pairs of formative comments. They found that while ChatGPT was similar to humans in how much rubric content was cited, it was less balanced in its attention to developmental appropriateness. It frequently recommended a level of lexical sophistication that surpassed learners' productive control. Hence, teachers continue to serve as indispensable mediators. They translate AI-generated suggestions into contextualised feedback and guard against over-correction that risks silencing the authorial voice. \n\nAs shown above, few studies have critically examined how AI-powered learner corpora function within actual primary classroom settings.",
