        "text": "There are several evidence-based approaches to teaching sight words to students. \n\nDirect instruction to teach reading could be done in different arrangements, such as one-on-one or group instruction. Some of the common strategies used by teachers in direct instruction include storytelling, sequencing, drilling, and scaffolding (Chitiyo, 2022). When practicing direct instruction, scaffolding has evidently proven to be a very effective strategy for students learning a language. When teachers scaffold in direct instruction, they may break down the content into fewer chunks to simplify the learning process for their students, or they may ask a variety of questions (e.g. who, where, what, why, how). When using scaffolding, the teacher's role is more of a facilitator that guides their students into interacting with text (Rupley et al., 2009). Mohr et al. (2024) highlighted three reading-to-writing practices to promote instruction. These practices are expanding reading-writing connections, gauging students' collaborative writing, and endorsing classroom writing community. Wang et al. (2024) conducted a systematic review to collect evidence to explore the impact and key elements of in-service special education teacher training in EBPs. They identified six main elements of training programs, namely, live modeling, didactic presentation, performance feedback, practical exercise, video modeling, and one-site coaching. Training programs that contain all or some of these elements showed an effectiveness level of 90% on teacher's outcomes. Kong et al. (2024) explored how early childhood educators/early interventionists look for evidence-based practices before implementing a practice and found that the most frequently used strategies among educators is the discussion with the colleagues. Garman-McClaine (2024) explored and compared the general education and special education teachers towards EBPS. They concluded that special education teachers have more positive attitudes toward EBPs. \n\nFrom the previous review of the literature, we can conclude that there is an unmet need to explore teachers' use of EBPs in teaching Arabic language for students with learning disabilities. Research related to exploring teachers' use of EBPs in language teaching for students with learning disabilities is limited either in the international or regional contexts.",
