      "snippet": {
        "text": "Sophisticated forms of creative content generated by Artificial Intelligence (AI) began to receive mainstream attention during 2022 when simple to operate applications emerged that could produce images that had highly convincing levels of 'humanness'.Although ChatGPT is not the first text-generating AI, it represented a significant breakthrough in generative AI (Gen-AI) due to its user-friendly chat-like interface.It quickly attracted mainstream attention as a novelty.This advancement led to optimistic forecasts about AI's application across multiple sectors, including medical, legal, and educational (Ahuja 2019;Grassini 2023), though it also very quickly provoked complaints that school and university students would be able to plagiarise essays (Crawford, Cowling, and Allen 2023;Oravec 2023).For example, it was shown to create plausible-sounding text in response to prompts such as: 'Explain the meaning of Shakespeare's Macbeth' (Huff 2023).OpenAI, the developers of ChatGPT, responded to these claims by suggesting that they would develop a kind of digital watermark that would enable readers to know if some text had been created with its platform (Hern 2022).Other writers were more tepid in their predictions (Grassini 2023;Kasneci et al. 2023;Metzler 2022), indicating that while there are obvious misuses for AI in education, it could be adopted to adjust how students learn and are assessed.\n\nIn this article, I will outline why the current -and next -generation of Gen-AI should be of interest to English teachers.I acknowledge there are some concerns with the technology, though I share McKnight's (2021) optimism that AI can be incorporated into the classroom in productive ways, such as introducing students to the practices of 'co-composing' with AI that will inevitably be part of their everyday lives in the workplace after leaving school (Yu 2023).My discussion here extends this work by foregrounding that there are opportunities to leverage the technology to improve outcomes in all aspects of subject English for students and teachers beyond writing instruction.",
