      "snippet": {
        "text": "Feedback is a critical component of the writing process (Hyland & Hyland, 2019), as it empowers learners to recognize and address their mistakes, ultimately improving their linguistic precision (Bitchener, 2019). Feedback also provides learners with explicit knowledge of language rules, contributing to long-term improvements in writing in a foreign language (see a review by Pawlak, 2013). Both teacher and peer feedback are integral to this process, offering distinct advantages. For instance, teacher feedback offers clear, actionable steps for improvement, which can motivate learners, boost their confidence, and encourage greater engagement and proactivity in learning to write (Hyland & Hyland, 2019). On the other hand, peer feedback helps learners develop evaluation skills while promoting a more profound comprehension of language use, structures, and conventions (Rouhi et al., 2020). \n\nHowever, teacher and peer feedback is not without limitations. Teacher feedback is often criticized for being too general, lacking the specificity needed to guide meaningful revisions (Zacharias, 2007). Peer feedback, meanwhile, tends to focus on surface-level corrections, such as grammar and word choice, rather than addressing deeper issues in content or structure (Guo et al., 2022). In light of these challenges, machine-generated feedback-particularly through tools like ChatGPT-has emerged as a promising alternative (Li, 2024;Teng, 2024a). ChatGPT, one of the most popular pre-trained large language models (LLMs), represents a significant advancement in generative AI (GenAI) technology. \n\nDespite its growing popularity, the impact of ChatGPT and similar technologies on writing remains relatively understudied. ChatGPT offers unique possibilities for enhancing educational experiences by providing interactive and personalized learning support (Hao et al., 2024). In particular, Yang et al. (2024) proposed that through the lens of collaborative poetic autoethnography, ChatGPT transcends its role as merely a tool or assistant; it has evolved into a pervasive \"ghostwriter\" that necessitates strategic engagement.",
