      "snippet": {
        "text": "Following the EduAbility cognitive taxonomy, we designed a suite of tasks targeting six hierarchical levels of educational ability: Memory, Understanding, Application, Reasoning, Creativity, and Ethics. Each task is categorized by its dominant cognitive level, although some tasks inevitably engage multiple levels of thinking. Our classification reflects the principal skill emphasized in the prompt and expected response, ensuring consistency across task design and evaluation. \n\nThe task set includes both data-derived and expert-constructed components. Tasks aligned with Memory, Understanding, Reasoning, and Application are primarily adapted from real-world educational materials, including national and regional exams (e.g., Gaokao, Zhongkao), textbook-based assessments, and authentic classroom transcripts. In contrast, Creativity and Ethics tasks are fully designed by expert annotators to capture open-ended generation, pedagogical planning, and moral reasoning in education-specific contexts. \n\nFor tasks requiring manual creation or postediting (e.g., classroom dialogue classification, ethical dilemmas, and all creativity tasks), we provide a high-level description of the construction workflow in this section. Annotators were trained in task-specific rubrics and followed standardized procedures to ensure validity and internal consistency. Each task is reviewed and validated by senior educators. Detailed prompt formats and example inputs are listed in Appendix C.4, and task-specific annotation guidelines are available in Appendix E.",
